From: FlipperEmu
Subject: [PATCH] eeprom_at24c: take a backing file that ends within a sector

The EEPROM wants its backing file to be exactly as long as the EEPROM.
The block layer gives an image's length in whole sectors, so a file of
256 bytes is 512 bytes long to the check, and so is one of 512 bytes:
no file can back an EEPROM that is smaller than a sector or ends within
one. That is every AT24C01 and AT24C02, the common small ones.

Compare with the EEPROM's size rounded up to a sector. Reading and
writing the EEPROM's own size works on a shorter file as it is: the
image reads as the sectors that hold it, and a write of 256 bytes at
the start leaves the file 256 bytes long.

Seen with the Flipper Zero machine, whose launcher keeps the contents
of an EEPROM on the GPIO header's I2C bus in a file of the device.

Signed-off-by: FlipperEmu
---
--- a/hw/nvram/eeprom_at24c.c
+++ b/hw/nvram/eeprom_at24c.c
@@ -174,7 +174,12 @@
     if (ee->blk) {
         int64_t len = blk_getlength(ee->blk);
 
-        if (len != ee->rsize) {
+        /*
+         * The length of an image comes in whole sectors: the file of an
+         * EEPROM that is smaller than one, or ends within one, is as long
+         * as the EEPROM, and reads as the sectors that hold it.
+         */
+        if (len != ROUND_UP(ee->rsize, BDRV_SECTOR_SIZE)) {
             error_setg(errp, "%s: Backing file size %" PRId64 " != %u",
                        TYPE_AT24C_EE, len, ee->rsize);
             return;
